    "lawCode": "Amd §§840 & 837, Exec L; amd §16, Ag & Mkts L",
    "lawSection": "Executive Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S5320\n\nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the executive law and the agriculture\nand markets law, in relation to police training regarding animal cruelty\nand protection laws\n\nPURPOSE:  The purpose of this bill is to improve the education and\ntraining of police officers in animal cruelty and protection laws and\nmake information about the laws readily available to police and prosecu-\ntors\n\nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one amends paragraph (k) of subdivision 1 of section 840 of the\nexecutive law to include the Department of Agriculture and Markets in\ndevelopment of police training policies and procedures by the Municipal\nPolice Training Council related to enforcement of animal cruelty and\nprotection laws. The Council would also make provisions for education\nand training in such policies and procedures.\n\nSection two amends section 837 of the executive law by adding a new\nsubdivision 20 that would require the Division of Criminal Justice\nServices to assist the Department of Agriculture and Markets in develop-\ning information about animal cruelty and protection laws to be used by\nlaw enforcement.\n\nSection three amends section 16 of the agriculture and markets law by\nadding a new subdivision 48 to make available both written and electron-\nic information about animal cruelty and protection laws for use by\npolice agencies, officers, and district attorneys and coordinate with\nthe Division of Criminal Justice Services to develop training for munic-\nipal police officers and district attorneys.\n\nSection four provides for the effective date.\n\nJUSTIFICATION:  Prevention, investigation and enforcement of animal\nabuse, cruelty and neglect have become a more substantial part of the\nwork of police officers. Although existing executive law for training\nmunicipal police officers includes language regarding the development of\npolicies and procedures pursuant to agricultural and markets animal\nprotection laws, there is a need to make it clear that education and\ntraining in these laws is a priority and that the training be developed\nwith input from the Department of Agriculture and Markets.  This bill\nwould update the executive law to achieve those goals.\n\nIn addition, because there have been reports that some police and prose-\ncutors may not be familiar with the agriculture and markets law, this\nbill would have the Department, in coordination with Division of Crimi-\nnal Justice Services, develop information about animal cruelty and\nprotection laws to enhance both the training and enforcement of these\nlaws. This information would be made readily available to police agen-\n\ncies and prosecutors to assist their daily work in protecting animals\nfrom abuse and cruel treatment.\n\nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY: This is a new bill.\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS: Undetermined.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:  This bill will take place immediately.\n\n",
